{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "age-04-parent-child-generation-relations",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Parent–Child and Generation Relationships",
    "description": "Solve age problems involving parents, children, and other generations at different dates.",
    "teachingApproach": "Use short, connected phone-sized teaching screens with concrete ages, labelled dates, separate calculation lines, a reason for each step, and an original-clue check. Place a fresh matching practice question after each worked example; wrong answers receive a worked correction and parallel practice.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 8, Problems on Ages. Approved scope: Teach parent/child and other generation problems that compare ages now with ages years ago or hence. Use a variable for the child or a shared multiple, express the older person's age consistently, and form the ratio or difference at the named date. Show how an age gap stays fixed and how a future ratio may become closer to one. Cover questions asking for the child's current age, a parent's current age, or the date when a stated ratio held. Check that the older/younger ordering and historical ages make sense. Learners should reason from the same relationship in both directions without confusing the age of a parent at a child's birth with the child's age now. Do not introduce demographic assumptions beyond those stated."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's solve age clues about parents, children, and grandparents.\n\nA parent may be three times a child's age today. As both grow older, that times-as-old statement can change. Let's follow the ages through time.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose a parent is four times a child's age today. Write the child as x and the parent as 4x.\n\nIn six years, both add six:\nChild: x + 6\nParent: 4x + 6.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If the parent will then be three times as old:\n4x + 6 = 3(x + 6)\n4x + 6 = 3x + 18\nx = 12.\n\nToday they are 12 and 48. In six years they are 18 and 54; 54 is three times 18. Notice that we added six to each age, not to the ratio.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
      "content": {
        "text": "A father is three times his child’s age now. In 10 years he will be twice the child’s age. Find the child’s present age.",
